        IF NOT `the court orders the accused to be present otherwise than by appearance by the designated counsel` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If the court orders the accused to be present otherwise than by appearance by the designated counsel, the court may" [F:may-if]
        IF step EQUALS `s 650.01(4) some other step` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "the court may (a) issue a summons ...; or (b) issue a warrant"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:13099 | 650.02 The prosecutor or the counsel designated under section 650.01 may appear before the court by audioconference or videoconference, if the technological means is satisfactory to the court.
@ref s 650.02 (src:13099-13099)
GIVEN `the person is the prosecutor or the counsel designated under section 650.01` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the technological means is satisfactory to the court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 650.02 the prosecutor or the designated counsel may appear before the court by audioconference or videoconference` MEANS
    IF `the person is the prosecutor or the counsel designated under section 650.01` AND `the technological means is satisfactory to the court`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may appear before the court by audioconference or videoconference, if the technological means is satisfactory to the court" [F:may-if]

-- src:13101 | 650.1 A judge in a jury trial may, before the charge to the jury, confer with the accused or counsel for the accused and the prosecutor with respect to the matters that should be explained to the jury and with respect to the choice of instructions to the jury.
@ref s 650.1 (src:13101-13101)
GIVEN `the trial is a jury trial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the charge to the jury has been given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 650.1 the judge may confer with the accused or counsel for the accused and the prosecutor about the matters to be explained to the jury and the instructions` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the trial is a jury trial` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A judge in a jury trial may"
        IF `the charge to the jury has been given` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may, before the charge to the jury, confer" (a period the text fixes in its own words: ruling (a) of 2026-11-05) [F:may-where] [M-a]
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 651 -- Summing up`

-- src:13103 | 651 (1) Where an accused, or any one of several accused being tried together, is defended by counsel, the counsel shall, at the end of the case for the prosecution, declare whether or not he intends to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused for whom he appears and if he does not announce his intention to adduce evidence, the prosecutor may address the jury by way of summing up.
@ref s 651(1) (src:13103-13103)
GIVEN `the accused is defended by counsel` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case for the prosecution has ended` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 651(1) counsel must declare whether or not they intend to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused` MEANS
    IF `the accused is defended by counsel` AND `the case for the prosecution has ended`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an accused ... is defended by counsel, the counsel shall, at the end of the case for the prosecution, declare whether or not he intends to adduce evidence" [M-a] [F:duty]

-- 651(1), the regulative twin: counsel for the accused must declare, by the end of the case for the prosecution, whether or not they intend to adduce evidence.
-- src:13103 | 651 (1) Where an accused, or any one of several accused being tried together, is defended by counsel, the counsel shall, at the end of the case for the prosecution, declare whether or not he intends to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused for whom he appears and if he does not announce his intention to adduce evidence, the prosecutor may address the jury by way of summing up.
@ref s 651(1) (src:13103-13103)
DECLARE `s 651(1) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 651(1) declare whether or not counsel intends to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused`

@ref s 651(1) (src:13103-13103)
GIVEN `the time of the end of the case for the prosecution` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 651(1) action`
`s 651(1) counsel must declare at the end of the case for the prosecution whether or not they intend to adduce evidence` MEANS
    PARTY `counsel for the accused`
    MUST `s 651(1) declare whether or not counsel intends to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused`
    WITHIN `the time of the end of the case for the prosecution`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BECAUSE "counsel for the accused did not declare, at the end of the case for the prosecution, whether or not counsel intends to adduce evidence"

-- src:13103 | 651 (1) Where an accused, or any one of several accused being tried together, is defended by counsel, the counsel shall, at the end of the case for the prosecution, declare whether or not he intends to adduce evidence on behalf of the accused for whom he appears and if he does not announce his intention to adduce evidence, the prosecutor may address the jury by way of summing up.
@ref s 651(1) (src:13103-13103)
GIVEN `the accused is defended by counsel` IS A BOOLEAN
      `counsel announces an intention to adduce evidence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 651(1) the prosecutor may address the jury by way of summing up` MEANS
    IF `the accused is defended by counsel` AND (NOT `counsel announces an intention to adduce evidence`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if he does not announce his intention to adduce evidence, the prosecutor may address the jury by way of summing up" [F:may-if]

-- src:13105 | (2) Counsel for the accused or the accused, where he is not defended by counsel, is entitled, if he thinks fit, to open the case for the defence, and after the conclusion of that opening to examine such witnesses as he thinks fit, and when all the evidence is concluded to sum up the evidence.
@ref s 651(2) (src:13105-13105)
DECLARE `s 651(2) the step` IS ONE OF
    `s 651(2) open the case for the defence`
    `s 651(2) examine such witnesses as they think fit, after the conclusion of the opening`
    `s 651(2) sum up the evidence, when all the evidence is concluded`
    `s 651(2) some other step`

-- src:13105 | (2) Counsel for the accused or the accused, where he is not defended by counsel, is entitled, if he thinks fit, to open the case for the defence, and after the conclusion of that opening to examine such witnesses as he thinks fit, and when all the evidence is concluded to sum up the evidence.
@ref s 651(2) (src:13105-13105)
GIVEN `the person is counsel for the accused, or the accused where not defended by counsel` IS A BOOLEAN
      step IS A `s 651(2) the step`
      `the opening has concluded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all the evidence is concluded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 651(2) counsel for the accused, or the accused where not defended by counsel, is entitled to take the step` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person is counsel for the accused, or the accused where not defended by counsel` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Counsel for the accused or the accused, where he is not defended by counsel, is entitled"
        IF step EQUALS `s 651(2) some other step` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "to open the case for the defence, and ... to examine such witnesses as he thinks fit, and ... to sum up the evidence"
        IF step EQUALS `s 651(2) examine such witnesses as they think fit, after the conclusion of the opening` AND (NOT `the opening has concluded`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "after the conclusion of that opening to examine such witnesses as he thinks fit" [F:entitled] [M-a]
        IF step EQUALS `s 651(2) sum up the evidence, when all the evidence is concluded` AND (NOT `all the evidence is concluded`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "when all the evidence is concluded to sum up the evidence" [F:entitled] [M-a]
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:13107 | (3) Where no witnesses are examined for an accused, he or his counsel is entitled to address the jury last, but otherwise counsel for the prosecution is entitled to address the jury last.
@ref s 651(3) (src:13107-13107)
DECLARE `s 651(3) who addresses the jury last` IS ONE OF
    `s 651(3) the accused or their counsel`
    `s 651(3) counsel for the prosecution`

-- src:13107 | (3) Where no witnesses are examined for an accused, he or his counsel is entitled to address the jury last, but otherwise counsel for the prosecution is entitled to address the jury last.
@ref s 651(3) (src:13107-13107)
GIVEN `no witnesses are examined for the accused` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 651(3) who addresses the jury last`
`s 651(3) who is entitled to address the jury last` MEANS
    IF `no witnesses are examined for the accused`
    THEN JUST `s 651(3) the accused or their counsel`
    ELSE JUST `s 651(3) counsel for the prosecution`

-- src:13109 | (4) Where two or more accused are tried jointly and witnesses are examined for any of them, all the accused or their respective counsel are required to address the jury before it is addressed by the prosecutor.
@ref s 651(4) (src:13109-13109)
GIVEN `two or more accused are tried jointly` IS A BOOLEAN
      `witnesses are examined for any of them` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 651(4) all the accused or their respective counsel must address the jury before it is addressed by the prosecutor` MEANS
    IF `two or more accused are tried jointly` AND `witnesses are examined for any of them`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where two or more accused are tried jointly and witnesses are examined for any of them, all the accused or their respective counsel are required to address the jury before it is addressed by the prosecutor" [M-a] [F:duty]

§ `Sections 652 to 654 -- View; jury retires; disagreement; mistrial`

-- src:13111 | 652 (1) The judge may, where it appears to be in the interests of justice, at any time after the jury has been sworn and before it gives its verdict, direct the jury to have a view of any place, thing or person, and shall give directions respecting the manner in which, and the persons by whom, the place, thing or person shall be shown to the jury, and may for that purpose adjourn the trial.
@ref s 652(1) (src:13111-13111)
GIVEN `it appears to be in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the jury has been sworn` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the jury has given its verdict` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 652(1) the judge may direct the jury to have a view of any place, thing or person` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `it appears to be in the interests of justice` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The judge may, where it appears to be in the interests of justice, at any time after the jury has been sworn and before it gives its verdict, direct the jury to have a view" [F:may-where] [M-a]
        IF (NOT `the jury has been sworn`) OR `the jury has given its verdict` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "at any time after the jury has been sworn and before it gives its verdict" (a period the text fixes in its own words: ruling (a) of 2026-11-05) [F:may-where] [M-a]
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:13111 | 652 (1) The judge may, where it appears to be in the interests of justice, at any time after the jury has been sworn and before it gives its verdict, direct the jury to have a view of any place, thing or person, and shall give directions respecting the manner in which, and the persons by whom, the place, thing or person shall be shown to the jury, and may for that purpose adjourn the trial.
@ref s 652(1) (src:13111-13111)
GIVEN `the judge directs the jury to have a view` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 652(1) the judge must give directions respecting the manner in which, and the persons by whom, the place, thing or person shall be shown to the jury` MEANS
    IF `the judge directs the jury to have a view`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "direct the jury to have a view of any place, thing or person, and shall give directions respecting the manner in which"

-- src:13113 | (2) Where a view is ordered under subsection (1), the judge shall give any directions that he considers necessary for the purpose of preventing undue communication by any person with members of the jury, but failure to comply with any directions given under this subsection does not affect the validity of the proceedings.
@ref s 652(2) (src:13113-13113)
GIVEN `a view is ordered under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge considers directions necessary for the purpose of preventing undue communication by any person with members of the jury` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 652(2) the judge must give the directions` MEANS
    IF `a view is ordered under subsection (1)` AND `the judge considers directions necessary for the purpose of preventing undue communication by any person with members of the jury`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a view is ordered under subsection (1), the judge shall give any directions that he considers necessary" [M-a] [F:duty]

-- src:13113 | (2) Where a view is ordered under subsection (1), the judge shall give any directions that he considers necessary for the purpose of preventing undue communication by any person with members of the jury, but failure to comply with any directions given under this subsection does not affect the validity of the proceedings.
@ref s 652(2) (src:13113-13113)
GIVEN `the failure is a failure to comply with directions given under subsection 652(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
