GIVEN `a view is ordered under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 652(3) the accused and the judge must attend the view` MEANS
    IF `a view is ordered under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a view is ordered under subsection (1), the accused and the judge shall attend" [M-a] [F:duty]

-- src:13117 | 652.1 (1) After the charge to the jury, the jury shall retire to try the issues of the indictment.
@ref s 652.1(1) (src:13117-13117)
GIVEN `the charge to the jury has been given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 652.1(1) the jury must retire to try the issues of the indictment` MEANS
    IF `the charge to the jury has been given`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "After the charge to the jury, the jury shall retire to try the issues of the indictment" (before the charge) [M-a] [F:duty]

-- (2): the number of cards the judge draws: none if 12 or fewer jurors remain, one if 13 remain, two if 14 remain. More than 14 is not covered (subsection 631(2.2) allows 13 or 14).
-- src:13119 | (2) However, if there are more than 12 jurors remaining, the judge shall identify the 12 jurors who are to retire to consider the verdict by having the number of each juror written on a card that is of equal size, by causing the cards to be placed together in a box that is to be thoroughly shaken together and by drawing one card if 13 jurors remain or two cards if 14 jurors remain. The judge shall then discharge any juror whose number is drawn.
@ref s 652.1(2) (src:13119-13119)
GIVEN `the number of jurors remaining` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 652.1(2) the number of cards the judge must draw to identify the 12 jurors who are to retire` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the number of jurors remaining` AT MOST 12 THEN JUST 0   -- a number, not a yes or no: the card-drawing step is "if there are more than 12 jurors remaining", so with 12 or fewer none is drawn
        IF `the number of jurors remaining` EQUALS 13 THEN JUST 1
        IF `the number of jurors remaining` EQUALS 14 THEN JUST 2
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection names 13 and 14 remaining jurors only

-- src:13121 | 653 (1) Where the judge is satisfied that the jury is unable to agree on its verdict and that further detention of the jury would be useless, he may in his discretion discharge that jury and direct a new jury to be empanelled during the sittings of the court, or may adjourn the trial on such terms as justice may require.
@ref s 653(1) (src:13121-13121)
DECLARE `s 653(1) the step` IS ONE OF
    `s 653(1) discharge that jury and direct a new jury to be empanelled during the sittings of the court`
    `s 653(1) adjourn the trial on such terms as justice may require`
    `s 653(1) some other step`

-- src:13121 | 653 (1) Where the judge is satisfied that the jury is unable to agree on its verdict and that further detention of the jury would be useless, he may in his discretion discharge that jury and direct a new jury to be empanelled during the sittings of the court, or may adjourn the trial on such terms as justice may require.
@ref s 653(1) (src:13121-13121)
GIVEN `the judge is satisfied that the jury is unable to agree on its verdict and that further detention of the jury would be useless` IS A BOOLEAN
      step IS A `s 653(1) the step`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 653(1) the judge may take the step` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the judge is satisfied that the jury is unable to agree on its verdict and that further detention of the jury would be useless` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the judge is satisfied that the jury is unable to agree on its verdict and that further detention of the jury would be useless, he may in his discretion discharge that jury" [F:may-where] [M-a]
        IF step EQUALS `s 653(1) some other step` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "he may in his discretion discharge that jury and direct a new jury to be empanelled during the sittings of the court, or may adjourn the trial"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:13123 | (2) A discretion that is exercised under subsection (1) by a judge is not reviewable.
@ref s 653(2) (src:13123-13123)
GIVEN `the discretion was exercised under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 653(2) the exercise of the discretion is reviewable` MEANS
    IF `the discretion was exercised under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A discretion that is exercised under subsection (1) by a judge is not reviewable" [M-a]
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A discretion that is exercised under subsection (1)"

-- src:13125 | 653.1 In the case of a mistrial, unless the court is satisfied that it would not be in the interests of justice, rulings relating to the disclosure or admissibility of evidence or the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms that were made during the trial are binding on the parties in any new trial if the rulings are made — or could have been made — before the stage at which the evidence on the merits is presented.
@ref s 653.1 (src:13125-13125)
GIVEN `there is a mistrial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rulings relate to the disclosure or admissibility of evidence or the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rulings are made, or could have been made, before the stage at which the evidence on the merits is presented` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that it would not be in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 653.1 the rulings made during the trial are binding on the parties in any new trial` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `there is a mistrial` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In the case of a mistrial"
        IF `the court is satisfied that it would not be in the interests of justice` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the court is satisfied that it would not be in the interests of justice" [F:unless] [M-a]
        IF `the rulings relate to the disclosure or admissibility of evidence or the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms` AND `the rulings are made, or could have been made, before the stage at which the evidence on the merits is presented` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "are binding on the parties in any new trial if the rulings are made — or could have been made — before the stage at which the evidence on the merits is presented" [M-a]

-- src:13127 | 654 The taking of the verdict of a jury and any proceeding incidental thereto is not invalid by reason only that it is done on Sunday or on a holiday.
@ref s 654 (src:13127-13127)
GIVEN `the proceeding is the taking of the verdict of a jury or a proceeding incidental to it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the only ground is that it is done on Sunday or on a holiday` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 654 the taking of the verdict, or a proceeding incidental to it, is invalid` MEANS
    IF `the proceeding is the taking of the verdict of a jury or a proceeding incidental to it` AND `the only ground is that it is done on Sunday or on a holiday`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is not invalid by reason only that it is done on Sunday or on a holiday" [M-a]
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "is not invalid by reason only that it is done on Sunday or on a holiday"
